# Trivy CRITICAL/HIGH exceptions. Every surviving entry below is backed by evidence recorded in
# docs/image-footprint.md (IMG-04 disposition table) — per-image presence/absence, installed
# version vs. the CVE's own documented fix threshold, and (where relevant) exactly why the
# runtime never reaches the vulnerable code path. Before writing a new entry here, or before
# trusting an existing one, verify it the same way: re-check the image and the upstream advisory
# directly rather than assuming a runtime-exposure or fix-availability claim still holds (see
# docs/cve-handling.md).

# pnpm: present in the API runtime image (corepack-installed, /usr/local/bin/pnpm, 10.34.5);
# absent from the web image entirely (no pnpm binary, no corepack cache — confirmed via
# `command -v pnpm` and a corepack-cache `find`, both negative on clokr-web:102-after). Not
# exploitable via the API's own runtime path: both CVEs require `pnpm install`/`pnpm add`
# resolving an attacker-influenced registry response or environment variable at install time.
# The API runtime never runs `pnpm install` — `docker-entrypoint.sh` has zero pnpm invocations of
# any kind; the only `pnpm install` in the whole Dockerfile is the BUILD-stage `deps` step
# (apps/api/Dockerfile:16), which does not exist in the shipped image. The sole runtime pnpm
# invocation is `pnpm tsx` — a run command against our own baked, trusted workspace
# (charts/clokr-app/templates/cronjob-anonymizer.yaml:51), not an install command.
# Corroborating, but deliberately not the primary reason (see docs/image-footprint.md's note on
# why a pnpm version-fix claim alone isn't trusted without independent confirmation): the
# installed pnpm 10.34.5 already exceeds both CVEs' GitHub-reviewed fix thresholds (10.27.0 for
# CVE-2025-69262, 10.26.0 for CVE-2025-69263 — GHSA-2phv-j68v-wwqx / GHSA-7vhp-vf5g-r2fw, both
# `github_reviewed_at`-confirmed, single clean version range each).
# Revisit: retire once policy allows relying on "Trivy silent + GHSA-confirmed fix version"
# without an additional corepack-cache absence proof, or when pnpm is dropped from the API
# image's retained toolchain entirely.
CVE-2025-69262
CVE-2025-69263

# pnpm: Repository-controlled configDependencies can select a malicious native
# install engine → arbitrary code execution (CVE-2026-55697 / GHSA-gj8w-mvpf-x27x).
# Present in the API image (pnpm@10.34.5), absent from web (see the block above for the
# per-image presence evidence). Not exploitable via the API's own runtime path: the exploit
# requires `pnpm install` to process attacker-controlled configDependencies. The production
# entrypoint never runs `pnpm install` (see above); the only pnpm invocation is `pnpm tsx`
# (a run command) against our own baked, trusted workspace. Altering configDependencies would
# require write access to the image filesystem (= full compromise already).
# CORRECTED 2026-08-21 (Phase 102 Plan 04): this entry previously stated "Fixed only in the pnpm
# 11.x line; NO backport to the 10.x line" as settled fact. GitHub's advisory record nominally
# lists a 10.x fix at >=10.34.2 (our installed 10.34.5 would exceed it), but the advisory itself
# carries unreviewed-candidate hallmarks — templated "CAND-PNPM-097" internal tooling text in its
# description, a "shared patch PR" hosted in a non-canonical companion repo
# (pnpm/pnpm-ghsa-j2hc-m6cf-6jm8, not pnpm/pnpm), and an explicit "set the final affected version
# range" listed as a still-open action item — and pnpm's own v10.34.2 GitHub release notes do NOT
# mention this fix (that release's only documented security fix is the unrelated
# GHSA-3qhv-2rgh-x77r, `.npmrc` environment-variable expansion). Treated as disputed, not relied
# upon; the entry stands on the runtime-exploitability argument above, independent of whether the
# version claim is real. The pnpm 11.x fix (>=11.5.3) is not in question, but bumping the
# monorepo to pnpm 11 would disturb the pnpm@10-specific Prisma hoisting in apps/api/Dockerfile
# (runtime stage) and is out of scope regardless (D-05, Phase 102).
# Revisit: confirm directly (a source diff against pnpm 10.34.2, or a reproduction attempt)
# whether the disputed 10.x backport is real; retire if confirmed. Otherwise revisit when the
# monorepo moves to pnpm 11.5.3+.
CVE-2026-55697

# brace-expansion: two ReDoS/DoS variants (CVE-2026-14257 fixed >=5.0.7, CVE-2026-13149 fixed
# >=5.0.6) plus a later bypass of the -14257 mitigation via unbounded intermediate arrays
# (CVE-2026-69152, fixed >=5.0.9 on the current release line / >=2.1.4 on the legacy-maintained
# 2.x line — confirmed against the npm registry's own "maintenance-v2" dist-tag, currently 2.1.4).
# Present at TWO different versions across the two images:
#   - npm's own bundle (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion, BOTH
#     images): 5.0.7 — fixes -14257 and -13149, does NOT fix -69152 (needs 5.0.9).
#   - the API image's corepack pnpm cache
#     (/root/.cache/node/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.5/dist/node_modules/brace-expansion): 2.1.2 —
#     does NOT fix -69152 (needs 2.1.4 on this line, confirmed against the npm registry: 2.1.2 is
#     two patch releases behind). GHSA's per-CVE data for -14257/-13149 gives only a single
#     top-line threshold with no published 2.x-line backport info, so 2.1.2 is conservatively
#     treated as still vulnerable to those two as well rather than assumed safe.
# Our app dependency tree resolves brace-expansion@5.0.9 (fixed for all three, via the root
# pnpm.overrides pin), so no application code path is affected — but this is a single global
# ignorefile covering both images, and the vulnerable copies above are genuinely present in build
# tooling that npm/corepack manage independently of app dependencies (`npm install -g npm@latest`
# and `corepack prepare pnpm@10.34.5` are unrelated to the pnpm.overrides pin and this phase does
# not touch either). Neither npm's nor pnpm's own install process is fed untrusted
# archives/registries at runtime in this image (see the pnpm blocks above — install never runs).
# Revisit: when npm bundles brace-expansion>=5.0.9 AND corepack's pnpm@10.34.5 dist bundle
# updates its own vendored copy to >=2.1.4 (or a later 2.x release, once published).
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-13149
CVE-2026-69152

# tar: uncontrolled recursion in mapHas/filesFilter -> uncatchable stack-overflow DoS via a
# crafted long-path GNU-L/PAX-x header, reachable only when tar.t()/tar.x() is called with a
# non-empty member-selection list against an attacker-supplied archive (CVE-2026-73566,
# GHSA-r292-9mhp-454m, high, github_reviewed_at 2026-07-24, single clean range: vulnerable
# <= 7.5.20, first_patched 7.5.21).
# This entry covers ONLY the corepack/pnpm vendor copy of tar. The other copy that used to
# carry this CVE (npm's own bundled tar, present in both images before this quick task) is
# GONE, not excepted: npm/npx are removed entirely from both runtime images (2026-08-25,
# quick-260825-qx4) because neither is ever invoked at runtime and npm@latest (checked at
# 12.0.2) still bundles the vulnerable tar@7.5.19 with no fix — updating npm no longer worked
# as a mitigation, so the unused component was deleted instead.
# Present (API image only): /root/.cache/node/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.5/dist/node_modules/tar
# -> 7.5.19, confirmed via `find` inside clokr-api:qx4-test. Absent from the web image
# entirely (`find / -path "*/node_modules/tar/package.json"` -> no hits) — web never runs
# corepack/pnpm at all.
# No fix exists on the pnpm 10.x line: pnpm 10.34.5 is the newest published 10.x release
# (confirmed against the npm registry's full version list, semver-sorted: 10.34.5 is the
# last entry before 11.0.0) and its own bundled tar is 7.5.19 (extracted directly from the
# `pnpm@10.34.5` npm tarball's dist/node_modules/tar/package.json, not inferred). pnpm's own
# 11.x line already carries the fix: pnpm@11.24.0 (the newest published 11.x release, same
# registry check) bundles tar@7.5.22, which exceeds the 7.5.21 threshold. Only a pnpm MAJOR
# bump (11.24.0+) would close this, which is out of scope here for the same reason as
# CVE-2026-55697 above (D-05, Phase 102): it would disturb the pnpm@10-specific Prisma
# hoisting in apps/api/Dockerfile's runtime stage.
# Not exploitable via the API's own runtime path: the vulnerable code (tar.t()/tar.x() with a
# non-empty member-selection list against untrusted archive bytes) requires pnpm's package-
# install/extraction machinery to run against an attacker-supplied tarball. The production
# runtime never does that — docker-entrypoint.sh has zero pnpm invocations at all, and the
# only pnpm invocation anywhere at runtime is `pnpm tsx scripts/anonymize-dump.ts`
# (charts/clokr-app/templates/cronjob-anonymizer.yaml:51), a run command against our own
# already-installed, lockfile-frozen workspace — no install/extract step, no external
# tarball. Verified dynamically, not just asserted: a Node `Module._resolveFilename` hook
# (`NODE_OPTIONS=--require <hook>.cjs`) was attached to a real `pnpm tsx --version`
# invocation inside clokr-api:qx4-test (same working directory as the CronJob,
# /app/apps/api); it logged 842 real module-resolution calls during that run and zero of
# them referenced `tar` in any form (confirmed the hook itself is live by first capturing
# and inspecting the full call log, not just grepping for an expected miss).
# Revisit: retire once pnpm publishes a 10.x release with tar>=7.5.21 (check the registry's
# semver-sorted 10.x tail, not just the latest tag), or when the monorepo moves to
# pnpm 11.24.0+ (D-05 territory — coordinate with the CVE-2026-55697 entry above, which has
# the same blocker).
CVE-2026-73566

# ip-address: SSRF/parsing DoS via leading-zero octet decimal/octal confusion (CVE-2026-69192,
# fixed >=10.3.1). The vulnerable copy (10.2.0) is present in npm's own bundle
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ip-address, pulled in via socks-proxy-agent) on
# BOTH images, and additionally in the API image's corepack pnpm cache
# (/root/.cache/node/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.5/dist/node_modules/ip-address) — neither location is
# fixed.
# CORRECTED 2026-08-21 (Phase 102 Plan 04): this entry previously claimed "our app dependency
# tree resolves ip-address@10.4.0" as the reason no runtime path is affected. Re-checked against
# the pruned image: the app tree (/app/node_modules) now has NO ip-address copy at all — the
# `--prod` prune dropped the devDependency chain (socks-proxy-agent and friends) that used to
# pull the pnpm.overrides-pinned fixed version in. That override (`"ip-address": ">=10.3.1"`)
# still exists in root package.json but is now moot for this specific package — nothing in the
# production dependency closure needs ip-address anymore, so it has no effect on the shipped
# image. The operative reason is instead that neither npm's socks-proxy-agent dependency nor
# pnpm's own bundled copy is ever exercised: no HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY/SOCKS proxy environment
# variable is configured anywhere in either Dockerfile, docker-entrypoint.sh, or any chart
# template (confirmed via a repo-wide search) — the vulnerable Address4 parsing path is only
# reachable through that unconfigured proxy feature.
# Revisit: when npm/corepack bundle ip-address>=10.3.1.
CVE-2026-69192
